Dear all,

Not really a methodological question, but rather an interpretation.
I modelled a couple of species roadkill through a dynamic occupancy model (using colext function) through a period of >10 years in the same sampled roads (divided into ~500 segments).
When retrieving mean values for colonization and extinction and plotting the results for each year (to explore the temporal component) I have an example where both the colonization and extinction present high values for the same year. The values are rather low (colonization ~ 0.4 and extinction ~0.2), but they are high when compared with other years. Does it make any sense that a species can present both colonization and extinction in the same year? I remember it is a mean value for each year including all ~500 sites.
I don't know if I have made myself clear.

Any assistance would be greatly appreciated.

From ubed9858844040a0b at groups.google.com  Wed Jul 19 16:36:53 2023
From: ubed9858844040a0b at groups.google.com (Quresh Latif)
Date: Wed, 19 Jul 2023 16:36:53 +0000
Subject: Re: Colonization and extinction having the same behaviour - colext
Message-ID: <WJm_CVKuaIk.a4aXncCnCAAJ@unmarked.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <WJm_CVKuaIk.lMg1W6mkCAAJ@unmarked.groups.google.com>
References: <WJm_CVKuaIk.lMg1W6mkCAAJ@unmarked.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/unmarked/c/WJm_CVKuaIk/m/a4aXncCnCAAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

If you have high colonization and high extinction, you effectively have high turnover. High turnover indicates the sites where species are located changed a lot in that year, i.e., they left the sites where they were in the previous year and colonized new sites, even if the total number of sites remained the same.

